WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:13.179
I never in a million years would have imagined&nbsp;that I would be

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:17.895
standing here today. The first experience that I had with Exxon

00:00:17.895 --> 00:00:20.410
was through&nbsp;a scholarship that they offered and I wrote an

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.229
essay about how I wanted to find substitutes&nbsp;for

00:00:23.229 --> 00:00:26.023
petroleum-based plastics; when I got the notification saying

00:00:26.023 --> 00:00:28.442
that I had been selected, I&nbsp;was blown out of the water.

00:00:28.442 --> 00:00:30.444
There's been so many opportunities that Exxon has provided

00:00:30.444 --> 00:00:34.239
me so I've&nbsp;actually spoken to members of Congress about what

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:37.492
it is like to be a woman in STEM. I was sitting&nbsp;next to the

00:00:37.492 --> 00:00:39.410
president of Emory yesterday, and so getting that kind of

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.706
interaction and being able&nbsp;to pick their brains is something that I

00:00:43.706 --> 00:00:46.542
think is a really cool opportunity.

00:00:46.542 --> 00:00:49.502
I really like the&nbsp;lab because it kind of takes that like passion for

00:00:49.502 --> 00:00:52.005
learning and then also that passion for doing&nbsp;things.

00:00:52.005 --> 00:00:55.510
There's a couple big projects, one is algae biofuels

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:59.011
and carbon capture sequestration, one&nbsp;thing that I think is

00:00:59.011 --> 00:01:01.513
really cool about Exxon is that the work that you're doing

00:01:01.513 --> 00:01:04.516
isn't just for&nbsp;yourself, it's really for the whole world and so

00:01:04.516 --> 00:01:06.518
hopefully I'll be part of one of those teams maybe&nbsp;someday.

00:01:06.518 --> 00:01:10.021
Being an engineer, sometimes bridging that gap of what you

00:01:10.021 --> 00:01:12.523
don't understand and taking&nbsp;it into the realm of it can

00:01:12.523 --> 00:01:15.984
be described by math and physics, but also truly appreciating

00:01:15.984 --> 00:01:18.612
the art&nbsp;behind it. Being at Exxon, you work with the best people,

00:01:18.612 --> 00:01:21.531
people who are diverse in thought and background.

00:01:21.531 --> 00:01:24.033
When you're new engineer, you might be afraid that you don't

00:01:24.033 --> 00:01:27.536
have the knowledge level, the&nbsp;experience to make a difference

00:01:27.536 --> 00:01:31.039
but here I've only been pushed to change the status quo.

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:34.501
As a child&nbsp;into my middle school and high school years, one

00:01:34.501 --> 00:01:37.003
of the things I really enjoyed doing was actually&nbsp;watching

00:01:37.003 --> 00:01:40.464
the History Channel. They would have these kind of special

00:01:40.464 --> 00:01:43.467
episodes that were titled engineering&nbsp;disasters and that's really

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:45.969
what started to get me into the field of safety.

00:01:45.969 --> 00:01:49.472
I never thought I&nbsp;would be in the shoes of somebody who has

00:01:49.472 --> 00:01:52.016
the responsibility of protecting our community's&nbsp;co-workers

00:01:52.016 --> 00:01:55.728
and our facilities, and being able to make an actual impact,

00:01:55.728 --> 00:02:01.024
an actual change in&nbsp;a way that I think makes a big difference

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.978
I saw an opportunity for an ocean engineering program,

00:02:03.978 --> 00:02:07.375
which I didn't really know what it meant but it seemed like it fit

00:02:07.375 --> 00:02:09.948
my love for being on the water,&nbsp;and wanting to do engineering.

00:02:09.948 --> 00:02:13.743
My senior year of undergrad I met somebody from ExxonMobil,

00:02:13.743 --> 00:02:16.708
came&nbsp;to watch the presentations and we struck up a

00:02:16.708 --> 00:02:20.247
conversation and he actually was a bit of a&nbsp;mentor to me,

00:02:20.247 --> 00:02:24.044
then following my internship, I was hired on full-time.

00:02:24.044 --> 00:02:26.672
Engineers and operations is&nbsp;a program that all of the upstream

00:02:26.672 --> 00:02:31.384
engineering new hires would go into, so essentially you&nbsp;are

00:02:31.384 --> 00:02:34.312
put out rotating to an operating asset where you're able

00:02:34.312 --> 00:02:38.921
to experience operations&nbsp;with mechanics, electricians,

00:02:38.921 --> 00:02:42.394
operators, and you don't have any engineering responsibilities.

00:02:42.394 --> 00:02:45.897
I think it set me up really well moving forward in my career and

00:02:45.897 --> 00:02:49.400
I just wanted to give that&nbsp;opportunity to other people and

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.903
coordinate the next year. It really allows me to see

00:02:52.903 --> 00:02:57.407
how what I do affects the corporation overall.

00:02:58.408 --> 00:03:01.911
The biggest thing about me is really around helping people.

00:03:01.911 --> 00:03:07.499
I never saw the energy industry as a place that I wanted to work

00:03:07.499 --> 00:03:11.021
I decided to do a summer&nbsp;experience and an internship with

00:03:11.021 --> 00:03:14.005
ExxonMobil at the Baytown chemical plants and

00:03:14.005 --> 00:03:16.651
I really loved that experience.

00:03:16.651 --> 00:03:19.260
The world is growing and we're gonna have 9 billion people

00:03:19.385 --> 00:03:24.260
in the world&nbsp;by 2040 and how do you supply energy to all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.604
these people. I think the work that we do here is&nbsp;important,

00:03:27.604 --> 00:03:32.188
It's always something new, and that variety

00:03:32.188 --> 00:03:34.700
in my work life is something that I love.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.361
I'm an&nbsp;upstream unconventionals reservoir engineering intern

00:03:39.361 --> 00:03:44.115
and I'm helping the team understand and&nbsp;constantly evaluate

00:03:44.115 --> 00:03:46.409
the Wells performance. Being on the forefront of some of these

00:03:46.409 --> 00:03:49.495
technologies&nbsp;and using them to really help assist you as

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:53.623
an engineer has really helped. About a month into&nbsp;the

00:03:53.623 --> 00:03:57.085
internship, my manager came to me and asked me if I'd like to

00:03:57.085 --> 00:04:01.589
go with the team to Canada&nbsp;and I was thrilled. Somehow they

00:04:01.589 --> 00:04:03.340
made it work for me which really meant a lot to me.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:07.177
I got to&nbsp;meet face to face some of the people in Canada that

00:04:07.177 --> 00:04:09.829
I always see their names on the morning&nbsp;calls and

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:12.640
say oh hi I'm the intern and this is what I'm working on,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.769
I just really felt like wow this team&nbsp;is kind of a family, if I commit

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:20.230
to a career with a company, I hope that they'd commit to me

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:23.733
as well&nbsp;and based on the people I've met here that is very true.

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:29.238
Growing up I participated in math and science&nbsp;competition

00:04:29.238 --> 00:04:33.742
sponsored by ExxonMobil. I actually took a trip as a kid

00:04:33.742 --> 00:04:39.247
to the ExxonMobil refinery, you&nbsp;know that gave me the dream

00:04:39.247 --> 00:04:42.708
or the longing to say in the future I want to be

00:04:42.708 --> 00:04:46.879
part of this company. Growing up in Nigeria, I witnessed

00:04:46.879 --> 00:04:51.382
the malaria initiative by ExxonMobil, all the kids that we

00:04:51.382 --> 00:04:54.510
have beneficiaries of that malaria initiative eventually went

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:58.013
to school and eventually became&nbsp;successful.

00:04:58.013 --> 00:05:02.517
The names on this wall inspires innovation to me, knowing

00:05:02.517 --> 00:05:06.771
that all these&nbsp;people were able to deliver tangible milestones

00:05:06.771 --> 00:05:10.775
for the company. I want to see my name on the&nbsp;wall knowing

00:05:10.775 --> 00:05:14.862
that I was part of this dynamic company that improves human

00:05:14.862 --> 00:05:18.390
lives through&nbsp;innovation and corporate social responsibility

